      "id": "SA-C57-shaknoti",
      "sequence": 2720,
      "title": "शक्नोति (śaknoti) — is able",
      "headword": "शक्नोति",
      "romanization": "śaknoti",
      "gloss": "is able — and it takes the -तुम् form you learnt two chapters ago",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:0f4aaa1e3190d188",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Build the past of पठति. Then say गन्तुम् and what its ending does."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: शक्नोति",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "शक्नोति (śaknoti) — \"is able, can\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Until now this book could say what somebody does and, since last chapter, what they did. It could not say what they could do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "शक्नोति (śaknoti) takes the -तुम् form as its object — which is why it arrives two chapters after that ending and not before:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "बालः गन्तुं शक्नोति bālaḥ gantuṁ śaknoti the boy can go."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Read the Sanskrit literally and it says the boy is-able to-go. English puts can in front of a bare verb; Sanskrit puts शक्नोति (śaknoti) at the end after an infinitive. Same idea, opposite order, and the infinitive is the piece that makes it possible."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You already know a word from this root without knowing it: शक्ति (śakti), \"power, capacity\" — the same शक्, made into a noun. English borrowed that one whole."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "(The म् of गन्तुम् is written ं here — gantuṁ — because a following consonant softens it. You have read that mark since the asking verb.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ability: what somebody is able to do, not merely what they do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
